  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Publish document change events from each Firestore to Pub/Sub and trigger Cloud Functions to apply the mutations to the peer project</b>.</p><p>This pattern uses Cloud Firestore triggers to react to document creates, updates, and deletes in each project, publishes a compact mutation to Pub/Sub, and then a subscriber function in the peer project applies the same change. It is event driven and pushes changes almost immediately, which makes meeting the under 15 seconds replication target realistic. You can run the Pub/Sub topic in a shared or peer project and grant cross project IAM to allow publish and subscribe. To prevent infinite write loops and to ensure idempotency, include an origin marker or version field and have the subscriber skip writes that originated from the other side or apply changes conditionally with transactions.</p><p>This approach also aligns with product capabilities. Cloud Functions for Firestore provides low latency triggers and Pub/Sub provides durable, scalable, and low latency delivery with at least once semantics, so you can add de duplication keys and retries to handle transient failures while still meeting the time requirement.</p><p><i>Schedule recurring gcloud firestore export and import operations with Cloud Scheduler every 30 minutes</i> does not satisfy the requirement because it is batch and coarse grained. It would deliver changes in minutes rather than seconds and it is not designed for continuous bidirectional replication.</p><p><i>Build a Cloud Data Fusion pipeline that reads from and writes to both Firestore instances for two way sync</i> is not suitable because Data Fusion is primarily an ETL service and does not provide native Firestore change capture with low latency propagation. Building conflict detection and loop avoidance would be complex and it would not reliably meet an under 15 seconds target.</p><p><i>Set up Datastream to capture changes from Firestore in each project and replicate them to the other Firestore</i> is incorrect because Datastream does not support Firestore as a source or as a destination. It is intended for change data capture from supported relational sources into supported sinks and Firestore is not among them.</p>",
